// HTTP/1.1, both directions, written over `net` and `io`.
//
// API -- the message
// ------------------
//   http.Error                       enum        why a message was refused
//   e.to_str(), e.status()                       the sentence, and the code to answer with
//
//   http.headers()                   -> Headers  empty, insertion-ordered
//   h.set(name, value), h.get(name), h.has(name), h.remove(name)
//   h.names()                        -> List<str>   lowercased, in order
//   h.size()                         -> int
//
//   http.parse_url(url)              -> Result<Url, Error>
//   u.scheme(), u.host(), u.port(), u.path(), u.query(), u.target()
//   u.host_header(), u.to_str()
//   http.quote(s, safe:)             -> str      percent-encode
//   http.unquote(s, plus:)           -> Result<str, Error>
//
//   http.request(method, url)        -> Result<Request, Error>
//   r.method(), r.url(), r.path(), r.query(), r.version(), r.header(n)
//   r.body(), r.text(), r.set(n, v), r.set_body(b), r.set_text(s),
//   r.set_chunked(on), r.set_version(v)
//
//   http.response(status)            -> Response
//   http.text_response(s, status:)   -> Response
//   http.json_response(v, status:)   -> Result<Response, Error>
//   p.status(), p.reason(), p.header(n), p.body(), p.text(), p.json()
//   p.set(n, v), p.set_body(b), p.set_text(s), p.set_chunked(on), p.set_reason(s)
//
// API -- the seam, over any `io.Stream`
// -------------------------------------
//   http.read_request(s)             -> Result<Option<Request>, Error>
//   http.write_request(s, req)       -> Result<void, Error>
//   http.read_response(s, method)    -> Result<Response, Error>
//   http.write_response(s, req, resp, keep_alive)  -> Result<void, Error>
//   http.should_keep_alive(req, resp) -> bool
//
// API -- the server
// -----------------
//   http.Handler                     interface   `Response handle(Request req)`
//   http.serve(listener, handler, connections:, timeout_ms:, max_requests:)
//                                    -> Result<int, Error>   connections served
//   http.serve_conn(conn, handler, max_requests:)
//                                    -> Result<int, Error>   requests served
//
// API -- the client
// -----------------
//   http.get(url)                    -> Result<Response, Error>
//   http.fetch(request, redirects:, timeout_ms:, trust:)  -> Result<Response, Error>
//                                    trust: a tls.Trust, for https (default: the system roots)
//
// **The seam takes an `io.Stream`, so every parser here is testable without a
// socket.** `read_request`, `write_request`, `read_response` and
// `write_response` never mention `net`: hand them an `io.Buffer` and they
// parse a transcript, hand them a `net.Conn` and they are a server or a
// client. Only `serve`, `serve_conn`, `get` and `fetch` know what a socket
// is, because only they have to set a timeout or make a connection. That is
// Oro's arrangement in `std/http.oro`, for Oro's reason: two parsers that
// agree today drift tomorrow, so there is one of each and the corpus drives
// it from memory.
//
// **A body is `bytes`, already read, and bounded by `MAX_BODY`.** There is no
// streaming body and no body reader object -- see "what is not here" below.
//
// --- the limits, and why each one exists -----------------------------------
//
// Every limit is a `pub const` a caller can read, print and compare against,
// because a limit that is a number in the middle of a function is a limit
// nobody can plan around. Each one bounds something an anonymous peer
// chooses:
//
//   MAX_HEAD          the whole header block, request line included
//   MAX_HEADERS       header fields in one block, and trailer fields after one
//   MAX_HEADER_LINE   one field line
//   MAX_CHUNK_LINE    one chunk size line
//   MAX_BODY          a body held in memory, in either direction
//   MAX_REDIRECTS     hops the client will follow
//   MAX_INTERIM       1xx responses stepped over before the peer is looping
//   MAX_REQUESTS      requests one kept-alive connection may carry
//   TIMEOUT_MS        how long a read or a write on a served connection waits
//
// A peer that passes one gets `Err(TooLarge(what, limit))`, whose message
// names both, and a server answers it 431.
//
// --- strictness: what is refused, and why ----------------------------------
//
// The rule is that **anything two parsers could read differently is
// refused**, because a request smuggled past a proxy is exactly a request
// the proxy and the server read differently. So:
//
//   - **Both `Content-Length` and `Transfer-Encoding`** is `Framing`, not a
//     resolution. RFC 9112 says prefer the latter; "says prefer" plus "the
//     proxy in front of me did not" is the whole attack.
//   - **A second `Content-Length` or `Transfer-Encoding`** is `Framing` for
//     the same reason, and a second `Host` is `Syntax`: neither field is
//     list-valued, so two of one is not a list.
//   - **A `Transfer-Encoding` that is not exactly `chunked`** is `Framing`.
//     `gzip, chunked` is well-formed and undecodable here, and handing back
//     coded bytes as if they were the body is the one answer that must not
//     happen.
//   - **Obsolete line folding** -- a field continued on an indented line --
//     is `Syntax`. RFC 9112 deprecated it and it is a smuggling vector.
//   - **A bare CR or LF inside the header block** is `Syntax`. The block is
//     split on CRLF, so a lone LF would otherwise leave a line that this
//     parser and the next one disagree about.
//   - **A NUL, a CR or a DEL anywhere in a field name or value** is
//     `Syntax`, naming the byte and where it was. A field name must be a
//     token (RFC 9110 `tchar`); a field value is HTAB and 0x20 upwards
//     except DEL.
//   - **A `Content-Length` that is not a non-empty run of ASCII digits** is
//     `Syntax`. That refuses `+5`, `0x5`, ` 5` and `5, 5` in one rule.
//   - **A chunk size that is not a non-empty run of hex digits** is
//     `Syntax`; no surrounding space is tolerated, and more than sixteen
//     digits is refused before the multiply can overflow. Chunk extensions
//     (`;name=value`) are skipped, and trailer fields are read and
//     discarded -- acting on a field that arrives after the body has been
//     handed to the application is a decision this module has no standing to
//     make.
//   - **An `HTTP/1.1` request with no `Host`** is `Syntax`. RFC 9112
//     requires it, and a virtual host that guesses is a virtual host that
//     can be aimed.
//   - **A leading empty line before a request** is refused, where RFC 9112
//     lets a recipient ignore one. It would have to be read out of the
//     stream before the header block, which means a second bounded read and
//     a second place the block can end; a peer that sends a stray CRLF after
//     its last message is malformed, and saying so is cheaper than carrying
//     the tolerance forever.
//   - **A request target** must be origin-form (`/path?query`) or
//     absolute-form (`http://host/path`, whose authority is dropped --
//     `Host` is what this module routes on). Asterisk-form (`*`) is accepted
//     only for `OPTIONS`. Authority-form, a relative target and a `#`
//     fragment are refused.
//
// --- duplicate header fields -----------------------------------------------
//
// **A repeated field is joined with `", "` in arrival order**, which is RFC
// 9110 §5.3: a list-valued field sent as several lines means the same as one
// line with the values comma-joined. `Host`, `Content-Length` and
// `Transfer-Encoding` are exempt and refused, above.
//
// The one field this rule is wrong for is **`Set-Cookie`**, which RFC 9110
// exempts because its value may contain a comma. This module has no cookie
// support at all -- no jar, no parsing, no `Set-Cookie` splitting -- so a
// caller who needs the individual values of several `Set-Cookie` lines does
// not get them here. That is a real gap and it is written down rather than
// papered over with a second accessor nothing else would use.
//
// `Headers` keeps **insertion order**, which is why it is two `List<str>`
// and not a `Map`: a map's order is its table's (§3.9), and a response whose
// header order changes between runs is a response no test can pin and no
// cache can key.
//
// --- what is deliberately not here -----------------------------------------
//
//   - **An unchecked `https`.** `https://` is supported, over `lib/tls.m31`,
//     and checked: every connection proves a certificate chain (or a pinned key)
//     before a byte of the request is sent, and there is no option, flag or
//     environment variable that skips it. The alternative -- opening a
//     plaintext connection to port 443, or accepting any certificate -- sends
//     in the clear, or to anyone, a request the caller believed was private.
//     What is checked and what is not (TLS 1.2, resumption, client
//     certificates and revocation are not) is in `lib/tls.m31`'s header.
//   - **A streaming body.** A body is read whole, into `bytes`, bounded by
//     `MAX_BODY`. A reader object would have to own its place in the
//     connection, which means a handler that does not read it desynchronises
//     the next request, which means a drain, which means a second limit --
//     and the shape that makes all of that go away is "the body is a value".
//     A file server and a proxy want the streaming shape, and neither is
//     here.
//   - **Cookies, authentication, pooling, retries and compression.** Each is
//     a policy the caller owns; Oro's `std/http.oro` lists the same five and
//     the reasons hold word for word.
//   - **A `Date` header.** RFC 9110 asks an origin server with a clock to
//     send one, and adding it would make every response a function of the
//     wall clock -- untestable, and a dependency on `date` for a field the
//     caller can set in one line.
//   - **`Expect: 100-continue`**, HTTP/2, HTTP/3, upgrades, `multipart`,
//     static file serving and routing.
//   - **`501` and `505`.** A refusal this module makes answers `400`, or
//     `431` for a limit. Splitting out "not implemented" and "version not
//     supported" means carrying a status through every error value, and the
//     message already says which it was. See `Error.status()`.

import io;
import json;
import net;
import tls;

// --- limits ----------------------------------------------------------------
/// The whole header block -- request or status line, every field, and the
/// blank line -- in bytes.
pub const int MAX_HEAD = 65536;
/// Header fields in one block, and trailer fields after a chunked body.
pub const int MAX_HEADERS = 100;
/// One field line, in bytes.
pub const int MAX_HEADER_LINE = 8192;
/// One chunk size line, extensions included, in bytes.
pub const int MAX_CHUNK_LINE = 32;
/// A body held in memory, in either direction, in bytes. 32 MiB.
pub const int MAX_BODY = 33554432;
/// How many redirects `fetch` follows before giving up.
pub const int MAX_REDIRECTS = 5;
/// How many interim (1xx) responses `read_response` steps over before
/// deciding the peer is looping. Nothing here asks for one -- there is no
/// `Expect: 100-continue` -- but `103 Early Hints` arrives unasked.
pub const int MAX_INTERIM = 8;
/// How many requests one kept-alive connection may carry before the server
/// hangs up and makes the client come back. It is fairness, not memory: it
/// stops a handful of trickling clients holding every slot of a server that
/// serves one connection at a time.
pub const int MAX_REQUESTS = 1000;
/// How long a read or a write on a served connection waits, in
/// milliseconds. 0 waits forever.
pub const int TIMEOUT_MS = 30000;
/// How large a piece of a body `set_chunked` writes as one chunk, and how
/// much is asked of a stream at a time.
pub const int CHUNK = 16384;
// The port an `http://` URL means when it does not say, and an `https://`
// one.
const int DEFAULT_PORT = 80;
const int DEFAULT_HTTPS_PORT = 443;
// Sent unless the caller sets their own: a surprising number of services
// answer 403 to a request without one.
const str USER_AGENT = "m31-http";
// The delimiters, as `bytes`. Module constants are immortal static data
// (§4.5), so naming one costs nothing at run time -- which matters for
// `CRLF`, which every line of every message is split on.
const bytes CRLF = [13, 10];
const bytes CRLF2 = [13, 10, 13, 10];
const bytes SP1 = [32];
const bytes COLON1 = [58];
const bytes SEMI1 = [59];
const bytes QUERY1 = [63];
const bytes HASH1 = [35];
const int HTAB = 9;
const int LF = 10;
const int CR = 13;
const int SP = 32;
const int PERCENT = 37;
const int PLUS = 43;
const int DEL = 127;
const str HEX = "0123456789abcdef";

// --- errors ----------------------------------------------------------------
/// Why a message was refused, or could not be exchanged.
///
/// The named variants are the ones a caller branches on -- "the peer sent
/// nonsense", "the framing is ambiguous", "it was too big", "that URL cannot
/// be requested", "it stopped in the middle", "the socket failed" -- and
/// `Other` is what keeps the enum from growing often: `match` is exhaustive
/// and has no default (§5.6), so a new variant breaks every program that
/// matches on an HTTP error. The five TLS variants were the one deliberate
/// exception -- a caller that sees `CertificateUntrusted` does something a
/// caller that sees `TlsFailed` does not.
///
/// Every variant carries a **sentence, not a code**. A refusal that a caller
/// cannot act on differently is a refusal they have to read, and the whole
/// point of being strict is being able to say exactly what was wrong.
///
/// Payload-free (docs/errors-decision.md §4): the sentence each variant used
/// to carry is context the caller already has -- the message, the URL, the
/// limit -- at the point it reports. The socket vocabulary that used to
/// nest a whole `net.Error` inside `Transport` is instead six variants of
/// this module's own, in `net`'s own names, built by `from_net` below --
/// each module's error type stays its own (§4), so this widens by name
/// rather than by nesting one enum inside another.
pub enum Error {
    /// The bytes are not a well-formed HTTP message.
    Syntax;
    /// The message's framing is ambiguous or names a coding this module does
    /// not speak. Separate from `Syntax` because it is the one class of
    /// refusal that is a security decision rather than a grammar one, and a
    /// gateway may want to count it.
    Framing;
    /// A limit this module publishes was passed.
    TooLarge;
    /// The URL cannot be requested at all -- no scheme, no host, a scheme
    /// that is neither `http` nor `https`.
    BadUrl;
    /// The peer stopped in the middle of a message.
    Closed;
    /// The connection was refused.
    Refused;
    /// The connection timed out.
    TimedOut;
    /// The host or network is unreachable.
    Unreachable;
    /// The connection was reset.
    Reset;
    /// The local address or port is already in use.
    InUse;
    /// The host name did not resolve.
    NameNotFound;
    /// A socket failure with none of the names above, or a refusal nobody
    /// named.
    Other;
    /// An `https` server's certificate does not lead to a trusted root, is
    /// not the pinned key, or breaks a rule of the chain.
    CertificateUntrusted;
    /// An `https` server's certificate, or one above it, is expired or not
    /// yet valid.
    CertificateExpired;
    /// An `https` server's certificate is not valid for the host asked for.
    HostnameMismatch;
    /// Anything else that stopped a TLS connection: a handshake the server
    /// failed, an alert, a record that did not verify, a stream cut without
    /// `close_notify`, a CA bundle that could not be read.
    TlsFailed;
    /// An `https` URL answered with a redirect to plain `http`. Never
    /// followed: the next request would go in the clear.
    InsecureRedirect;
}

/// A sentence saying what went wrong. A method named `to_str`, so `print(e)`
/// prints it.
pub str Error.to_str() {
    match (this) {
        case Syntax: {
            return "the message is not well-formed HTTP";
        }
        case Framing: {
            return "the message's framing is ambiguous or unsupported";
        }
        case TooLarge: {
            return "a limit this module publishes was passed";
        }
        case BadUrl: {
            return "that URL cannot be requested";
        }
        case Closed: {
            return "the peer stopped in the middle of a message";
        }
        case Refused: {
            return "the connection was refused";
        }
        case TimedOut: {
            return "the connection timed out";
        }
        case Unreachable: {
            return "the host or network is unreachable";
        }
        case Reset: {
            return "the connection was reset";
        }
        case InUse: {
            return "the local address or port is already in use";
        }
        case NameNotFound: {
            return "the host name did not resolve";
        }
        case Other: {
            return "the request failed";
        }
        case CertificateUntrusted: {
            return "the server's certificate is not trusted";
        }
        case CertificateExpired: {
            return "the server's certificate has expired or is not yet valid";
        }
        case HostnameMismatch: {
            return "the server's certificate is not valid for that host";
        }
        case TlsFailed: {
            return "the TLS connection failed";
        }
        case InsecureRedirect: {
            return "an https URL redirected to plain http";
        }
    }
}

/// The status a server answers this refusal with.
///
/// Four hundred for everything the peer got wrong, 431 for a limit, 500 for
/// a failure that was this end's. There is deliberately no 501 and no 505:
/// distinguishing them would mean carrying a status inside every error
/// value, and `to_str()` already says which coding or which version was
/// refused. See the module note.
pub int Error.status() {
    match (this) {
        case Syntax: {
            return 400;
        }
        case Framing: {
            return 400;
        }
        case TooLarge: {
            return 431;
        }
        case BadUrl: {
            return 400;
        }
        case Closed: {
            return 400;
        }
        case Refused: {
            return 500;
        }
        case TimedOut: {
            return 500;
        }
        case Unreachable: {
            return 500;
        }
        case Reset: {
            return 500;
        }
        case InUse: {
            return 500;
        }
        case NameNotFound: {
            return 500;
        }
        case Other: {
            return 500;
        }
        case CertificateUntrusted: {
            return 500;
        }
        case CertificateExpired: {
            return 500;
        }
        case HostnameMismatch: {
            return 500;
        }
        case TlsFailed: {
            return 500;
        }
        case InsecureRedirect: {
            return 500;
        }
    }
}

// An `io.Error` off a stream, in this module's vocabulary. It goes through
// `net.error_of`, which is exact for everything a socket produces, so a
// timeout arrives as `Error.TimedOut` and not as a number.
Error transport(io.Error e) {
    return from_net(net.error_of(e));
}

// `net.Error`, in this module's own vocabulary -- each module's error type
// stays its own (docs/errors-decision.md §4), so a socket failure widens by
// matching on `net`'s names and returning this module's, rather than nesting
// a whole `net.Error` inside this one.
Error from_net(net.Error e) {
    match (e) {
        case Refused: {
            return Error.Refused;
        }
        case TimedOut: {
            return Error.TimedOut;
        }
        case Unreachable: {
            return Error.Unreachable;
        }
        case Reset: {
            return Error.Reset;
        }
        case InUse: {
            return Error.InUse;
        }
        case NameNotFound: {
            return Error.NameNotFound;
        }
        case Other(int code): {
            return Error.Other;
        }
    }
}

// `tls.Error`, in this module's own vocabulary, for the same reason as
// `from_net`. The three a caller acts on differently -- an untrusted
// certificate, an expired one, one for another host -- keep their own names;
// the rest of the thirty-odd reasons a TLS connection can stop are
// `TlsFailed`, and `tls`'s `last_error()` is where the exact one lives for a
// caller who holds the connection. The socket's own failures, which `tls`
// reports as `Connect`, never reach here: `exchange` opens the socket itself.
Error from_tls(tls.Error e) {
    match (e) {
        case Connect: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case Io: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case Truncated: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case PeerAlert: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case Record: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case BadRecordMac: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case Closed: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case UnsupportedVersion: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case MissingExtendedMasterSecret: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case BadRenegotiationInfo: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case Downgrade: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case RetryRequested: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case IllegalParameter: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case UnsupportedExtension: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case Malformed: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case UnexpectedMessage: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case ClientCertificateRequested: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case ClientCertificateUnusable: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case BadCertificate: {
            return Error.CertificateUntrusted;
        }
        case UntrustedKey: {
            return Error.CertificateUntrusted;
        }
        case TrustStore: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case UnknownAuthority: {
            return Error.CertificateUntrusted;
        }
        case CertificateExpired: {
            return Error.CertificateExpired;
        }
        case CertificateNotYetValid: {
            return Error.CertificateExpired;
        }
        case HostnameMismatch: {
            return Error.HostnameMismatch;
        }
        case InvalidChain: {
            return Error.CertificateUntrusted;
        }
        case WeakCertificate: {
            return Error.CertificateUntrusted;
        }
        case UnsupportedSignature: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case BadSignature: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
        case BadFinished: {
            return Error.TlsFailed;
        }
    }
}

// --- byte classes ----------------------------------------------------------
// RFC 9110's grammar, written as predicates over one octet. They are the
// whole of this module's strictness, so each is the RFC's rule and nothing
// near it.

// tchar: what a method, a field name and a chunk extension name may contain.
bool is_tchar(int c) {
    if (c >= '0' && c <= '9' || c >= 'a' && c <= 'z' || c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z') {
        return true;
    }
    return c == '!' || c == '#' || c == '$' || c == '%' || c == '&' || c == '\'' || c == '*' || c == '+' || c == '-' || c == '.' || c == '^' || c == '_' || c == '`' || c == '|' || c == '~';
}

// A non-empty run of tchars.
bool is_token(bytes b) {
    if (b.size() == 0) {
        return false;
    }
    for (int c in b) {
        if (!is_tchar(c)) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}

// The first byte of `b` that may not appear in a field value, as an index,
// or -1. HTAB is allowed; everything below 0x20 is not, DEL is not, and
// bytes above 127 are -- they are answered for by the UTF-8 decode that
// turns a value into a `str`.
int bad_value_at(bytes b) {
    for (int i in 0 .. b.size()) {
        int c = b[i];
        if (c != HTAB && (c < SP || c == DEL)) {
            return i;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}

// The first byte of `b` that may not appear in a request target, or -1:
// visible ASCII and above, so a control character or a space is refused and
// raw UTF-8 in a path -- which clients do send -- is not.
int bad_target_at(bytes b) {
    for (int i in 0 .. b.size()) {
        int c = b[i];
        if (c <= SP || c == DEL) {
            return i;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}

int hex_val(int c) {
    if (c >= '0' && c <= '9') {
        return c - 48;
    }
    if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'f') {
        return c - 87;
    }
    if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'F') {
        return c - 55;
    }
    return -1;
}

// A non-negative int as lowercase hex with no leading zeros: a chunk size.
str hex_of(int v) {
    if (v == 0) {
        return "0";
    }
    List<str> digits = [];
    int n = v;
    while (n > 0) {
        int d = n & 15;
        digits.push(HEX.substr(d, d + 1));
        n = n >> 4;
    }
    digits.reverse();
    return digits.join("");
}

// How a message names one byte: the character if it is printable ASCII, and
// the hex otherwise -- a byte of a multi-byte UTF-8 character is not a
// character on its own, and a NUL has no spelling at all.
str shown(int c) {
    if (c == 0) {
        return "a NUL byte";
    }
    if (c == CR) {
        return "a carriage return";
    }
    if (c == LF) {
        return "a line feed";
    }
    if (c >= '!' && c <= '~') {
        bytes one = [c];
        return "'" + one.utf8().or("?") + "'";
    }
    bytes one = [c];
    return "the byte 0x" + one.hex();
}

// How a message quotes a run of bytes that may not be text and may be long:
// the text in quotes if every byte is printable ASCII and there are few
// enough of them, and hex otherwise. An error message is written to a log,
// and a log that echoes whatever a client sent is a log nobody can read.
str quoted(bytes b) {
    bytes head = b;
    str tail = "";
    if (head.size() > 48) {
        head = b.substr(0, 48);
        tail = "...";
    }
    for (int i in 0 .. head.size()) {
        int c = head[i];
        if (c < ' ' || c > '~') {
            return "0x" + head.hex() + tail;
        }
    }
    return "'" + head.utf8().or("") + tail + "'";
}

// Bytes this module has already checked are printable ASCII, as text. The
// `or` is never taken: every caller has run `is_token`, `bad_value_at` or
// `bad_target_at` over these bytes first.
str ascii(bytes b) {
    return b.utf8().or("");
}

// --- headers ---------------------------------------------------------------
/// The field lines of one message: lowercased names, in the order they
/// arrived or were set.
///
/// **Two lists and not a `Map`.** A map's iteration order is its table's and
/// changes when it rehashes (§3.9), so a response built from a map would put
/// its fields in a different order between runs -- which no corpus test can
/// pin, no cache can key, and no reader of a transcript can follow. A
/// hundred fields is the limit, so a linear scan is the right shape besides.
///
/// Names are folded to lowercase once, when they go in, so every lookup is a
/// comparison and no caller has to remember which spelling arrived.
pub type Headers {
    List<str> ns;
    List<str> vs;
}

/// Set `name` to `value`, replacing whatever was there.
///
/// The name is folded to lowercase. Nothing is checked here: a name or a
/// value that cannot be sent is caught by `write_request` and
/// `write_response`, which answer with an `Error` naming the field, because
/// a header built out of a request's own data is data and not a bug in the
/// program.
pub void Headers.set(str name, str value) {
    str key = name.to_lower();
    match (ns.index_of(key)) {
        case Some(int i): {
            vs[i] = value;
        }
        case None: {
            ns.push(key);
            vs.push(value);
        }
    }
}

/// The value of `name`, or `None`. The name is matched case-insensitively.
pub Option<str> Headers.get(str name) {
    match (ns.index_of(name.to_lower())) {
        case Some(int i): {
            return Option<str>.Some(vs[i]);
        }
        case None: {
            return Option<str>.None;
        }
    }
}

/// Whether `name` is present.
pub bool Headers.has(str name) {
    return ns.contains(name.to_lower());
}

/// Remove `name` if it is there.
pub void Headers.remove(str name) {
    match (ns.index_of(name.to_lower())) {
        case Some(int i): {
            str gone = ns.remove_at(i);
            str also = vs.remove_at(i);
        }
        case None: {
        }
    }
}

/// The field names, lowercased, in order. A fresh list.
pub List<str> Headers.names() {
    return clone(ns);
}

/// How many fields there are.
pub int Headers.size() {
    return ns.size();
}

// A copy, so that a `Request` handed to a handler cannot have its fields
// changed under the caller that built it.
Headers Headers.copy() {
    return Headers(clone(ns), clone(vs));
}

// The parser's door: add a field that has just been read off the wire.
//
// A repeat is joined with ", ", which is RFC 9110 §5.3 -- except for the
// three fields that are not list-valued, where a repeat is refused. See the
// module note on duplicates.
Result<void, Error> Headers.merge(str name, str value) {
    match (ns.index_of(name)) {
        case Some(int i): {
            if (name == "content-length" || name == "transfer-encoding") {
                return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Framing);
            }
            if (name == "host") {
                return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
            }
            vs[i] = vs[i] + ", " + value;
        }
        case None: {
            ns.push(name);
            vs.push(value);
        }
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

// Append `name: value\r\n` for every field, having checked that each can be
// sent. This is response splitting on the way out and request splitting on
// the way in, and it is the same check in both directions -- `kind` is in
// the message and nowhere else, because a client told that its *request*
// field is an illegal *response* field looks in the wrong place.
Result<void, Error> Headers.render(bytes out, str kind) {
    for (int i in 0 .. ns.size()) {
        bytes name = ns[i].to_bytes();
        bytes value = vs[i].to_bytes();
        if (!is_token(name)) {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
        int bad = bad_value_at(value);
        if (bad >= 0) {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
        out.extend(name);
        out.push(':');
        out.push(SP);
        out.extend(value);
        out.extend(CRLF);
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

/// An empty `Headers`.
pub Headers headers() {
    List<str> ns = [];
    List<str> vs = [];
    return Headers(ns, vs);
}

// Whether a comma-separated field holds `token`, case-insensitively: the
// question `Connection: keep-alive, Upgrade` is asked.
bool has_token(Option<str> field, str token) {
    match (field) {
        case Some(str v): {
            for (str part in v.to_lower().split(",")) {
                if (part.trim() == token) {
                    return true;
                }
            }
            return false;
        }
        case None: {
            return false;
        }
    }
}

// --- percent-encoding ------------------------------------------------------
// RFC 3986 §2.3's unreserved set, written out rather than assembled because
// it is a quotation: a reader checking this against the RFC should read the
// same sixty-six characters in both places.
const str UNRESERVED = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789-._~";

/// `s` with every octet outside the unreserved set, and outside `safe`,
/// written as `%HH`.
///
/// `safe` is the extra characters to leave alone, and it differs by where
/// the text is going: `"/"` for a path, `""` for one query value, and a
/// caller who wants `?q=a&b` -- a value containing an `&` -- must say
/// `quote(v, safe: "")` and get it right. The unreserved characters are
/// never escaped whatever `safe` says: `%41` and `A` name the same
/// character, so an encoder that escapes what it need not is an encoder
/// whose output nobody can compare.
///
/// The digits are uppercase, which RFC 3986 §6.2.2.1 says to prefer.
pub str quote(str s, str safe = "/") {
    List<str> out = [];
    for (int c in s.to_bytes()) {
        bytes one = [c];
        str ch = "";
        if (c >= ' ' && c <= '~') {
            ch = ascii(one);
        }
        if (ch != "" && (UNRESERVED.contains(ch) || safe.contains(ch))) {
            out.push(ch);
        } else {
            out.push("%" + HEX.substr(c >> 4, (c >> 4) + 1).to_upper() + HEX.substr(c & 15, (c & 15) + 1).to_upper());
        }
    }
    return out.join("");
}

/// The text `s` spells with its `%HH` escapes decoded.
///
/// `plus: true` also reads `+` as a space, which is the form a query string
/// and an HTML form body use and a path does not. It is a flag on the one
/// operation rather than a second name because it is one decoder with one
/// extra byte mapped.
///
/// **A malformed escape is an error, not a literal `%`.** A `%` that is not
/// followed by two hex digits means the text was built by something that did
/// not encode it, and reading it through unchanged is how two readers
/// disagree about what a path was. Escapes that spell bytes which are not
/// UTF-8 are refused too, because the answer is a `str`.
pub Result<str, Error> unquote(str s, bool plus = false) {
    bytes raw = pct_decode(s.to_bytes(), plus, "the text")?;
    match (raw.utf8()) {
        case Some(str t): {
            return Result<str, Error>.Ok(t);
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<str, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
    }
}

// The octets `b` spells. `where` names the part of the message in the error,
// so "the request target" and "the URL" say which.
Result<bytes, Error> pct_decode(bytes b, bool plus, str where) {
    bytes out = [];
    int i = 0;
    int n = b.size();
    while (i < n) {
        int c = b[i];
        if (c == PERCENT) {
            if (i + 2 >= n) {
                return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
            }
            int hi = hex_val(b[i + 1]);
            int lo = hex_val(b[i + 2]);
            if (hi < 0 || lo < 0) {
                return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
            }
            out.push(hi * 16 + lo);
            i = i + 3;
        } else if (plus && c == PLUS) {
            out.push(SP);
            i = i + 1;
        } else {
            out.push(c);
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }
    return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(out);
}

// --- URLs ------------------------------------------------------------------
/// An absolute `http://` or `https://` URL, taken apart.
///
/// Made by `parse_url`, or by this module out of a request's target and its
/// `Host` field -- which is why a `Request` read off a socket answers
/// `url()` with the URL the client asked for, and not only with a path.
pub type Url {
    str sch;
    str hst;
    int prt;
    str pth;
    str qry;
    str tgt;
}

/// The scheme, lowercased: `"http"` or `"https"`. Nothing else parses.
pub str Url.scheme() {
    return sch;
}

/// The host: a name, or an IP literal with no brackets.
pub str Url.host() {
    return hst;
}

/// The port, filled in with 80 (`http`) or 443 (`https`) when the URL did
/// not say.
pub int Url.port() {
    return prt;
}

/// The path, **percent-decoded**, and never empty -- a URL with no path has
/// the path `/`.
///
/// Decoding is what a router wants and it loses one distinction: `%2F` comes
/// out as `/`, so `/a%2Fb` and `/a/b` have the same `path()`. That is the
/// same answer Oro's `std/http.oro` gives and the same one `urllib` gives,
/// and it is stated here rather than left to be discovered, because a router
/// for which the difference matters must read `target()`, which is the
/// escapes exactly as they arrived.
pub str Url.path() {
    return pth;
}

/// Everything after the first `?`, **not** decoded: splitting a query into
/// pairs is the caller's, because only the caller knows whether a `;` is a
/// separator and whether a `+` is a space. `unquote(v, plus: true)` is the
/// other half.
pub str Url.query() {
    return qry;
}

/// The request target, byte for byte as it will be sent: the path and query
/// exactly as they were written, with their escapes intact.
pub str Url.target() {
    return tgt;
}

/// The form the `Host` field wants: the authority with the default port left
/// off, because RFC 9110 says a client should omit it and a server that
/// virtual-hosts on the field should see `example.com` and not
/// `example.com:80`. An IPv6 literal is bracketed.
pub str Url.host_header() {
    if (prt == default_port(sch)) {
        return bracket(hst);
    }
    return bracket(hst) + ":" + prt.to_str();
}

/// The URL as text, which `parse_url` reads back.
///
/// The asterisk target has no URL form -- `OPTIONS *` asks about the server
/// and not about a resource -- so it prints as the authority alone. An
/// HTTP/1.0 request that sent no `Host` has no authority either, and prints
/// as its target alone, which is all that was ever said about where it went.
pub str Url.to_str() {
    if (hst == "") {
        return tgt;
    }
    if (tgt == "*") {
        return sch + "://" + host_header();
    }
    return sch + "://" + host_header() + tgt;
}

// The port a scheme means when a URL does not say.
int default_port(str scheme) {
    if (scheme == "https") {
        return DEFAULT_HTTPS_PORT;
    }
    return DEFAULT_PORT;
}

// The index of the first byte of `b` that is `a` or `c`, or -1. Two needles
// and not `index_of` twice, because the answer is the earlier of the two and
// finding both to compare them walks the string again.
int first_of(bytes b, int a, int c) {
    for (int i in 0 .. b.size()) {
        if (b[i] == a || b[i] == c) {
            return i;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}

// An IPv6 literal is nothing but colons, so it is bracketed everywhere it is
// written next to a port -- which is what `net.Addr.to_str` does too.
str bracket(str host) {
    if (host.contains(":")) {
        return "[" + host + "]";
    }
    return host;
}

/// Parse an absolute `http://` or `https://` URL.
///
///     http.parse_url("http://example.com:8080/a/b?x=1#frag")
///
/// A relative URL is refused: a client with no base to resolve against
/// cannot make one absolute, and guessing a scheme is how a request goes
/// somewhere nobody asked for. A fragment is parsed and dropped -- it names
/// a place in the answer and RFC 9110 says it is never sent.
///
/// **`https://` is spoken over TLS, and never quietly downgraded.** Whether
/// a URL is `http` or `https` is kept on the `Url` and decides, in `fetch`,
/// whether the request is written to the socket or to a verified TLS
/// connection over it. The failure this module exists to prevent is the
/// quiet one: a plaintext connection to port 443, or a certificate nobody
/// checked, carrying a credential the caller believed was private. So there
/// is no scheme guessing, no `http` retry after an `https` failure, and no
/// way to switch verification off.
///
/// Userinfo before the host (`http://user:pw@host/`) is refused: RFC 9110
/// deprecates it and this client will not send it.
pub Result<Url, Error> parse_url(str url) {
    bytes b = url.to_bytes();
    match (b.index_of("://".to_bytes())) {
        case Some(int sep): {
            return after_scheme(url, b, sep);
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<Url, Error>.Err(Error.BadUrl);
        }
    }
}

Result<Url, Error> after_scheme(str url, bytes b, int sep) {
    str scheme = ascii(b.substr(0, sep)).to_lower();
    if (scheme != "http" && scheme != "https") {
        return Result<Url, Error>.Err(Error.BadUrl);
    }
    bytes rest = b.substr(sep + 3, b.size());
    // The fragment is cut before anything else looks at the bytes, so a `#`
    // cannot be mistaken for part of a query.
    match (rest.index_of(HASH1)) {
        case Some(int h): {
            rest = rest.substr(0, h);
        }
        case None: {
        }
    }
    // The authority ends at the first `/` or `?` (RFC 3986 §3.2), so
    // `http://host?q=1` is a host and a query and not a host with a question
    // mark in its name -- which is what `urlsplit` answers too. A URL with no
    // path at all gets `/`, because that is the target that will be sent.
    bytes authority = rest;
    bytes target = "/".to_bytes();
    int cut = first_of(rest, 47, 63);
    if (cut >= 0) {
        authority = rest.substr(0, cut);
        target = rest.substr(cut, rest.size());
        if (target[0] != '/') {
            bytes with_root = "/".to_bytes();
            with_root.extend(target);
            target = with_root;
        }
    }
    if (authority.contains("@".to_bytes())) {
        return Result<Url, Error>.Err(Error.BadUrl);
    }
    if (bad_target_at(target) >= 0) {
        return Result<Url, Error>.Err(Error.BadUrl);
    }
    int fallback = default_port(scheme);
    List<str> hp = split_host_port(authority, quoted(b), fallback)?;
    return url_of(scheme, hp[0], hp[1].parse_int().or(fallback), target, quoted(b));
}

// Build a `Url` out of a host, a port and a target. Shared by `parse_url` and
// by the server's request reader, because the two differ only in where the
// authority came from -- a URL says it, a request puts it in `Host`, and an
// HTTP/1.0 request may not say at all, which is the one case where the host
// is empty.
Result<Url, Error> url_of(str scheme, str host, int port, bytes target, str what) {
    str path = ascii(target);
    str query = "";
    match (target.index_of(QUERY1)) {
        case Some(int q): {
            path = ascii(target.substr(0, q));
            query = ascii(target.substr(q + 1, target.size()));
        }
        case None: {
        }
    }
    if (path != "*") {
        bytes decoded = pct_decode(path.to_bytes(), false, what)?;
        match (decoded.utf8()) {
            case Some(str t): {
                path = t;
            }
            case None: {
                return Result<Url, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
            }
        }
    }
    return Result<Url, Error>.Ok(Url(scheme, host, port, path, query, ascii(target)));
}

// `host`, `host:port`, `[::1]` or `[::1]:port`, as a two-element list of the
// host and the port in decimal. The scan for the port's colon starts after
// the closing bracket, for the reason `net`'s own does: an IPv6 literal is
// nothing but colons, and the last one before the bracket is not a port
// separator. A list rather than two returns, which the language has not got.
Result<List<str>, Error> split_host_port(bytes authority, str what, int fallback) {
    if (authority.size() == 0) {
        return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.BadUrl);
    }
    int colon = -1;
    if (authority[authority.size() - 1] == ']') {
        colon = -1;
    } else {
        int i = authority.size() - 1;
        while (i >= 0) {
            int c = authority[i];
            if (c == ']') {
                i = -1;
            } else if (c == ':') {
                colon = i;
                i = -1;
            } else {
                i = i - 1;
            }
        }
    }
    bytes host = authority;
    str port = fallback.to_str();
    if (colon >= 0) {
        host = authority.substr(0, colon);
        bytes digits = authority.substr(colon + 1, authority.size());
        if (digits.size() == 0 || digits.size() > 5) {
            return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.BadUrl);
        }
        for (int c in digits) {
            if (c < '0' || c > '9') {
                return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.BadUrl);
            }
        }
        port = ascii(digits);
        int n = port.parse_int().or(0);
        if (n < 1 || n > 65535) {
            return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.BadUrl);
        }
    }
    if (host.size() >= 2 && host[0] == '[' && host[host.size() - 1] == ']') {
        host = host.substr(1, host.size() - 1);
    }
    if (host.size() == 0) {
        return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.BadUrl);
    }
    match (host.utf8()) {
        case Some(str h): {
            List<str> out = [h.to_lower(), port];
            return Result<List<str>, Error>.Ok(out);
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.BadUrl);
        }
    }
}

// --- status reasons --------------------------------------------------------
// The codes this module has a phrase for. A constant `Map<int, str>` is
// static data laid out by the compiler (§4.5), so this costs nothing at run
// time. Anything else gets the class's name, which is what RFC 9110 says a
// recipient should fall back to.
const Map<int, str> REASONS = {100: "Continue", 101: "Switching Protocols", 200: "OK", 201: "Created", 202: "Accepted", 203: "Non-Authoritative Information", 204: "No Content", 205: "Reset Content", 206: "Partial Content", 300: "Multiple Choices", 301: "Moved Permanently", 302: "Found", 303: "See Other", 304: "Not Modified", 307: "Temporary Redirect", 308: "Permanent Redirect", 400: "Bad Request", 401: "Unauthorized", 403: "Forbidden", 404: "Not Found", 405: "Method Not Allowed", 406: "Not Acceptable", 408: "Request Timeout", 409: "Conflict", 410: "Gone", 411: "Length Required", 412: "Precondition Failed", 413: "Content Too Large", 414: "URI Too Long", 415: "Unsupported Media Type", 416: "Range Not Satisfiable", 417: "Expectation Failed", 418: "I'm a teapot", 421: "Misdirected Request", 422: "Unprocessable Content", 426: "Upgrade Required", 428: "Precondition Required", 429: "Too Many Requests", 431: "Request Header Fields Too Large", 451: "Unavailable For Legal Reasons", 500: "Internal Server Error", 501: "Not Implemented", 502: "Bad Gateway", 503: "Service Unavailable", 504: "Gateway Timeout", 505: "HTTP Version Not Supported"};

/// The reason phrase for a status code: the registered one, or the name of
/// its class.
pub str reason(int status) {
    match (REASONS.get(status)) {
        case Some(str r): {
            return r;
        }
        case None: {
        }
    }
    if (status >= 100 && status < 200) {
        return "Informational";
    }
    if (status >= 200 && status < 300) {
        return "Successful";
    }
    if (status >= 300 && status < 400) {
        return "Redirection";
    }
    if (status >= 400 && status < 500) {
        return "Client Error";
    }
    return "Server Error";
}

// 1xx, 204 and 304 have no body and no framing fields, ever. A
// Content-Length on a 304 is a real interoperability bug and not a harmless
// one: a client that believes it goes looking for a body that is not coming.
bool bodiless(int status) {
    return status >= 100 && status < 200 || status == 204 || status == 304;
}

// --- requests --------------------------------------------------------------
/// One request, in either direction: the one a client is about to send, and
/// the one a server has just read.
///
/// It carries a whole `Url` rather than a bare target, so a handler can ask
/// `req.url().host()` without going back to the `Host` field, and a client's
/// redirect logic has somewhere to resolve against. A request read off a
/// socket gets its URL from its target and its `Host`.
pub type Request {
    str verb;
    Url loc;
    str ver;
    Headers head;
    bytes payload = [];
    bool framed = false;
}

/// The method, as it was written: `"GET"`, `"POST"`. HTTP methods are
/// case-sensitive, so nothing is folded.
pub str Request.method() {
    return verb;
}

/// The URL this request is for.
pub Url Request.url() {
    return loc;
}

/// The percent-decoded path -- `loc.path()`, which is what a handler routes
/// on.
pub str Request.path() {
    return loc.path();
}

/// The raw query string, `loc.query()`.
pub str Request.query() {
    return loc.query();
}

/// `"1.0"` or `"1.1"`.
pub str Request.version() {
    return ver;
}

/// A header field, matched case-insensitively.
pub Option<str> Request.header(str name) {
    return head.get(name);
}

/// Every field name, lowercased, in order.
pub List<str> Request.header_names() {
    return head.names();
}

/// The body, exactly. Empty when there was none.
pub bytes Request.body() {
    return clone(payload);
}

/// The body as text, or `Err(Syntax(..))` if it is not UTF-8.
pub Result<str, Error> Request.text() {
    return utf8_of(payload, "the request body");
}

/// The body, parsed as JSON.
pub Result<json.Value, Error> Request.json() {
    return json_of(payload, "the request body");
}

/// Set a header field, replacing whatever was there.
pub void Request.set(str name, str value) {
    head.set(name, value);
}

/// Replace the body.
pub void Request.set_body(bytes b) {
    payload = clone(b);
}

/// Replace the body with the UTF-8 of `s`.
pub void Request.set_text(str s) {
    payload = s.to_bytes();
}

/// Send this body with `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` instead of a
/// `Content-Length`.
///
/// The body is still held whole in memory, so this buys nothing in space; it
/// is a **framing choice**, for a peer that wants chunks and for the tests
/// that drive the decoder. A streaming body -- the thing chunked framing
/// exists for -- is not in this module; see the module note.
pub void Request.set_chunked(bool on) {
    framed = on;
}

/// Send `"1.0"` or `"1.1"` on the request line. Anything else traps: it is
/// the program's own value, and there is no other version this module can
/// speak.
pub void Request.set_version(str v) {
    if (v != "1.0" && v != "1.1") {
        trap("set_version(" + v + "): this module speaks HTTP/1.0 and HTTP/1.1");
    }
    ver = v;
}

/// A request to send: the method, and an absolute `http://` or `https://` URL.
///
///     http.request("GET", "http://127.0.0.1:8080/health")
///
/// The version is 1.1 and the body is empty; `set_body`, `set_text` and
/// `set` fill in the rest. A method that is not a token is refused here
/// rather than at the write, so a typo does not reach a socket.
pub Result<Request, Error> request(str method, str url) {
    if (!is_token(method.to_bytes())) {
        return Result<Request, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    Url u = parse_url(url)?;
    return Result<Request, Error>.Ok(Request(method, u, "1.1", headers()));
}

// --- responses -------------------------------------------------------------
/// One response: the one a handler is building, and the one a client has
/// just read.
pub type Response {
    int code;
    str phrase;
    str ver;
    Headers head;
    bytes payload = [];
    bool framed = false;
}

/// The status code.
pub int Response.status() {
    return code;
}

/// The reason phrase: the peer's when this was read off a socket, and the
/// registered one otherwise.
pub str Response.reason() {
    return phrase;
}

/// `"1.0"` or `"1.1"`, as the peer wrote it.
pub str Response.version() {
    return ver;
}

/// A header field, matched case-insensitively.
pub Option<str> Response.header(str name) {
    return head.get(name);
}

/// Every field name, lowercased, in order.
pub List<str> Response.header_names() {
    return head.names();
}

/// The body, exactly.
pub bytes Response.body() {
    return clone(payload);
}

/// The body as text, or `Err(Syntax(..))` if it is not UTF-8.
///
/// It does **not** look at `Content-Type`: a charset in a header is what the
/// peer claims, and what the bytes are is what `utf8()` says. A body that
/// is not UTF-8 is refused rather than repaired, for the reason
/// `bytes.utf8()` gives (§3.10).
pub Result<str, Error> Response.text() {
    return utf8_of(payload, "the response body");
}

/// The body, parsed as JSON.
///
/// It composes with `lib/json.m31` at the one place they meet: the body is
/// decoded as UTF-8 first, because `json.parse` takes a `str` and RFC 8259
/// requires the document to be UTF-8, and a parse failure comes back as
/// `Syntax` carrying `json.Error`'s own sentence -- which already names the
/// byte position.
pub Result<json.Value, Error> Response.json() {
    return json_of(payload, "the response body");
}

/// Set a header field, replacing whatever was there.
pub void Response.set(str name, str value) {
    head.set(name, value);
}

/// Replace the body.
pub void Response.set_body(bytes b) {
    payload = clone(b);
}

/// Replace the body with the UTF-8 of `s`.
pub void Response.set_text(str s) {
    payload = s.to_bytes();
}

/// Write this body as chunks rather than with a `Content-Length`. See
/// `Request.set_chunked`: it is a framing choice, not a streaming one.
pub void Response.set_chunked(bool on) {
    framed = on;
}

/// Send `r` as the reason phrase instead of the registered one.
pub void Response.set_reason(str r) {
    phrase = r;
}

/// An empty response with this status and the registered reason phrase.
///
/// A status outside 100..599 traps: it is the program's own number, and
/// there is no status line to write for it.
pub Response response(int status) {
    if (status < 100 || status > 599) {
        trap("response(" + status.to_str() + "): a status is 100..599");
    }
    return Response(status, reason(status), "1.1", headers());
}

/// A `text/plain; charset=utf-8` response carrying `body`.
pub Response text_response(str body, int status = 200) {
    Response r = response(status);
    r.set("content-type", "text/plain; charset=utf-8");
    r.set_text(body);
    return r;
}

/// An `application/json` response carrying `v`.
///
/// It is a `Result` because `json.stringify` is: a NaN, an infinity and a
/// value that contains itself have no JSON spelling.
pub Result<Response, Error> json_response(json.Value v, int status = 200) {
    match (json.stringify(v)) {
        case Ok(str text): {
            Response r = response(status);
            r.set("content-type", "application/json");
            r.set_text(text);
            return Result<Response, Error>.Ok(r);
        }
        case Err(json.Error e): {
            return Result<Response, Error>.Err(Error.Other);
        }
    }
}

Result<str, Error> utf8_of(bytes b, str what) {
    match (b.utf8()) {
        case Some(str t): {
            return Result<str, Error>.Ok(t);
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<str, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
    }
}

Result<json.Value, Error> json_of(bytes b, str what) {
    str text = utf8_of(b, what)?;
    match (json.parse(text)) {
        case Ok(json.Value v): {
            return Result<json.Value, Error>.Ok(v);
        }
        case Err(json.Error e): {
            return Result<json.Value, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
    }
}

// --- reading a message head ------------------------------------------------
// The header block, up to and including the blank line, or an empty `bytes`
// if the stream was at a clean end before anything arrived.
Result<bytes, Error> read_head(io.Stream s) {
    match (s.read_until(CRLF2, MAX_HEAD)) {
        case Ok(bytes raw): {
            if (raw.size() == 0 || raw.ends_with(CRLF2)) {
                return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(raw);
            }
            if (raw.size() >= MAX_HEAD) {
                return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.TooLarge);
            }
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Closed);
        }
        case Err(io.Error e): {
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(transport(e));
        }
    }
}

// The head's lines, without the blank one that ends it. A bare CR or LF
// inside any of them is refused: the block was split on CRLF, so a lone LF
// would leave a line this parser and the next one disagree about, which is
// the whole of request smuggling.
Result<List<bytes>, Error> head_lines(bytes raw) {
    List<bytes> lines = raw.substr(0, raw.size() - 4).split(CRLF);
    for (int i in 0 .. lines.size()) {
        bytes line = lines[i];
        if (line.size() > MAX_HEADER_LINE) {
            return Result<List<bytes>, Error>.Err(Error.TooLarge);
        }
        for (int k in 0 .. line.size()) {
            if (line[k] == CR || line[k] == LF) {
                return Result<List<bytes>, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
            }
        }
    }
    return Result<List<bytes>, Error>.Ok(lines);
}

// Every field line from `from` to the end of the block.
Result<Headers, Error> parse_headers(List<bytes> lines, int from) {
    Headers h = headers();
    int i = from;
    while (i < lines.size()) {
        if (i - from >= MAX_HEADERS) {
            return Result<Headers, Error>.Err(Error.TooLarge);
        }
        bytes line = lines[i];
        i = i + 1;
        if (line.size() == 0) {
            // The block is split on CRLF, so an empty line inside it is a
            // message that ended and then went on -- which the read above
            // cannot produce, and which is refused rather than ignored.
            return Result<Headers, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
        if (line[0] == SP || line[0] == HTAB) {
            return Result<Headers, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
        parse_header_line(h, line)?;
    }
    return Result<Headers, Error>.Ok(h);
}

Result<void, Error> parse_header_line(Headers h, bytes line) {
    int colon = -1;
    match (line.index_of(COLON1)) {
        case Some(int c): {
            colon = c;
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
    }
    bytes name = line.substr(0, colon);
    bytes value = line.substr(colon + 1, line.size());
    if (!is_token(name)) {
        if (name.size() == 0) {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
        int bad = 0;
        while (bad < name.size() && is_tchar(name[bad])) {
            bad = bad + 1;
        }
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    int badv = bad_value_at(value);
    if (badv >= 0) {
        return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    // Only SP and HTAB can be here now, so `trim` is exactly the optional
    // whitespace RFC 9110 puts either side of a field value.
    bytes trimmed = value.trim();
    match (trimmed.utf8()) {
        case Some(str v): {
            return h.merge(ascii(name).to_lower(), v);
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
    }
}

// --- bodies ----------------------------------------------------------------
// Exactly `n` bytes. A stream that ends first is the peer having declared a
// length it did not send, which is a lie and not a short answer: on the way
// in it desynchronises the connection, and on the way out it is the JSON
// document the caller is about to parse with half of it missing.
Result<bytes, Error> read_exact(io.Stream s, int n, str what) {
    bytes out = [];
    while (out.size() < n) {
        int want = n - out.size();
        if (want > CHUNK) {
            want = CHUNK;
        }
        match (s.read(want)) {
            case Ok(bytes got): {
                if (got.size() == 0) {
                    return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Closed);
                }
                out.extend(got);
            }
            case Err(io.Error e): {
                return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(transport(e));
            }
        }
    }
    return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(out);
}

// Everything until the stream ends, up to `limit`.
Result<bytes, Error> read_to_end(io.Stream s, int limit, str what) {
    bytes out = [];
    while (true) {
        match (s.read(CHUNK)) {
            case Ok(bytes got): {
                if (got.size() == 0) {
                    return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(out);
                }
                out.extend(got);
                if (out.size() > limit) {
                    return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.TooLarge);
                }
            }
            case Err(io.Error e): {
                return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(transport(e));
            }
        }
    }
}

// One CRLF-terminated line, without its CRLF.
Result<bytes, Error> read_line(io.Stream s, int limit, str what) {
    match (s.read_until(CRLF, limit)) {
        case Ok(bytes got): {
            if (got.ends_with(CRLF)) {
                return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(got.substr(0, got.size() - 2));
            }
            if (got.size() >= limit) {
                return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.TooLarge);
            }
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Closed);
        }
        case Err(io.Error e): {
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(transport(e));
        }
    }
}

/// A `Transfer-Encoding: chunked` body, decoded.
///
/// A size line, that many bytes, a CRLF, and round again; a size of zero
/// ends it and is followed by the trailer fields, which are read and
/// discarded. Chunk extensions after a `;` are skipped. `limit` bounds the
/// decoded body, because a chunked body declares no total and a peer that
/// keeps sending chunks is otherwise a request for all of memory.
Result<bytes, Error> read_chunked(io.Stream s, int limit) {
    bytes out = [];
    while (true) {
        bytes line = read_line(s, MAX_CHUNK_LINE, "a chunk size line")?;
        int size = chunk_size(line)?;
        if (size == 0) {
            skip_trailer(s)?;
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Ok(out);
        }
        if (out.size() + size > limit) {
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.TooLarge);
        }
        out.extend(read_exact(s, size, "a chunk")?);
        bytes term = read_exact(s, 2, "a chunk terminator")?;
        if (term[0] != CR || term[1] != LF) {
            return Result<bytes, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
    }
}

// A chunk size line: hex digits, then optional `;ext=...` this module
// ignores. No surrounding whitespace is tolerated -- RFC 9112 does not put
// any there, and a decoder that strips it is one a smuggler can aim.
Result<int, Error> chunk_size(bytes line) {
    bytes digits = line;
    match (line.index_of(SEMI1)) {
        case Some(int semi): {
            digits = line.substr(0, semi);
        }
        case None: {
        }
    }
    if (digits.size() == 0) {
        return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    // Fifteen hex digits, and not sixteen, because the multiply below **traps
    // on overflow** (§6.1) and sixteen digits reach 2^64-1, which an `int`
    // cannot hold. Fifteen reach 2^60-1, a thousand times the largest body
    // this module will assemble, so nothing legitimate is refused and a
    // hostile size is an error value rather than a dead process.
    if (digits.size() > 15) {
        return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    int v = 0;
    for (int c in digits) {
        int d = hex_val(c);
        if (d < 0) {
            return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
        v = v * 16 + d;
    }
    return Result<int, Error>.Ok(v);
}

// Trailer fields are read and discarded. Acting on a field that arrives
// after the body has already been handed to the application is a decision
// this module has no standing to make -- and a trailer that could change
// what the body meant is exactly the thing a cache in front of it would not
// have seen.
Result<void, Error> skip_trailer(io.Stream s) {
    int count = 0;
    while (true) {
        bytes line = read_line(s, MAX_HEADER_LINE, "a trailer line")?;
        if (line.size() == 0) {
            return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
        }
        count = count + 1;
        if (count > MAX_HEADERS) {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(Error.TooLarge);
        }
    }
}

// A `Content-Length` value: a non-empty run of ASCII digits and nothing
// else. That one rule refuses `+5`, `0x5`, ` 5`, `5 ` and the `5, 5` that a
// duplicated field would have been joined into.
Result<int, Error> content_length(str v) {
    bytes digits = v.to_bytes();
    if (digits.size() == 0) {
        return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    // Eighteen digits and not nineteen: nineteen can spell a number an `int`
    // cannot hold, and `parse_int` would answer `None` -- which an `or(0)`
    // would turn into "no body", the one wrong answer available. Eighteen
    // digits is 10^18, thirty billion times `MAX_BODY`.
    if (digits.size() > 18) {
        return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    for (int c in digits) {
        if (c < '0' || c > '9') {
            return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
    }
    match (v.parse_int()) {
        case Some(int n): {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(n);
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
    }
}

// Which of the two framing fields is in force, and what they agree on.
// Answers -2 for chunked, -1 for "no body at all", and a length otherwise.
//
// A message carrying both is refused rather than resolved. That ambiguity is
// the classic request-smuggling vector: whichever this end picks, some proxy
// in front of it picks the other, and the disagreement is an injected
// message.
Result<int, Error> framing(Headers h) {
    match (h.get("transfer-encoding")) {
        case Some(str te): {
            if (h.has("content-length")) {
                return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.Framing);
            }
            if (te.to_lower() != "chunked") {
                return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.Framing);
            }
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(-2);
        }
        case None: {
        }
    }
    match (h.get("content-length")) {
        case Some(str n): {
            int len = content_length(n)?;
            if (len > MAX_BODY) {
                return Result<int, Error>.Err(Error.TooLarge);
            }
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(len);
        }
        case None: {
        }
    }
    return Result<int, Error>.Ok(-1);
}

// --- reading a request -----------------------------------------------------
/// The next request on `s`, or `None` at a clean end -- the peer closed
/// between messages, which on a kept-alive connection is how it says it is
/// finished.
///
/// The body is read here, whole, so the request handed on is a value and the
/// stream is positioned at the next message. A request with neither framing
/// field has **no body**: not "read until the end", which on a kept-alive
/// connection would read the next request as this one's body -- the same
/// smuggling bug from the other direction.
pub Result<Option<Request>, Error> read_request(io.Stream s) {
    bytes raw = read_head(s)?;
    if (raw.size() == 0) {
        return Result<Option<Request>, Error>.Ok(Option<Request>.None);
    }
    List<bytes> lines = head_lines(raw)?;
    List<str> start = request_line(lines[0])?;
    Headers h = parse_headers(lines, 1)?;
    str version = start[2];
    str host = "";
    match (h.get("host")) {
        case Some(str v): {
            host = v;
        }
        case None: {
            // RFC 9112 requires it of 1.1, and a virtual host that guesses is
            // a virtual host that can be aimed. A 1.0 request may leave it
            // out, and then the URL has no host.
            if (version == "1.1") {
                return Result<Option<Request>, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
            }
        }
    }
    Url loc = target_url(start[0], start[1], host)?;
    Request req = Request(start[0], loc, version, h);
    int len = framing(h)?;
    if (len == -2) {
        req.payload = read_chunked(s, MAX_BODY)?;
    } else if (len > 0) {
        req.payload = read_exact(s, len, "the request body")?;
    }
    return Result<Option<Request>, Error>.Ok(Option<Request>.Some(req));
}

// The method, the target and the version, as three strings.
Result<List<str>, Error> request_line(bytes line) {
    if (line.size() == 0) {
        return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    // Exactly one space either side of the target: tolerating runs of spaces
    // is how a request line becomes two different requests to two readers.
    List<bytes> parts = line.split(SP1);
    if (parts.size() != 3) {
        return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    if (!is_token(parts[0])) {
        return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    int bad = bad_target_at(parts[1]);
    if (bad >= 0) {
        return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    if (parts[1].size() == 0) {
        return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    str version = version_of(parts[2], "request line")?;
    List<str> out = [ascii(parts[0]), ascii(parts[1]), version];
    return Result<List<str>, Error>.Ok(out);
}

Result<str, Error> version_of(bytes field, str where) {
    if (!field.starts_with("HTTP/".to_bytes())) {
        return Result<str, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    bytes rest = field.substr(5, field.size());
    str v = ascii(rest);
    if (v != "1.0" && v != "1.1") {
        // `quoted` and not `v`: a version field that is not UTF-8 decodes to
        // the empty string, and "unsupported HTTP version ''" says nothing
        // about what actually arrived.
        return Result<str, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    return Result<str, Error>.Ok(v);
}

// The URL a request target and a `Host` field name together.
//
// Origin-form (`/p?q`) is the ordinary one. Absolute-form
// (`http://host/p`) is accepted because RFC 9112 requires a server to accept
// it, and its authority is dropped: `Host` is what this module routes on,
// and a request that says two different hosts must not be resolved into one.
// Asterisk-form is accepted for `OPTIONS` alone. Everything else --
// authority-form, a relative target, a fragment -- is refused.
Result<Url, Error> target_url(str method, str target, str host) {
    bytes t = target.to_bytes();
    str what = "the request target " + quoted(t);
    // An HTTP/1.0 request may arrive with no `Host` at all, and then there is
    // no authority to put in the URL: the host is empty and the port is the
    // default. Every other path here has one, because 1.1 must send it.
    str name = "";
    int port = DEFAULT_PORT;
    if (host != "") {
        List<str> hp = split_host_port(host.to_bytes(), "the Host header field " + quoted(host.to_bytes()), DEFAULT_PORT)?;
        name = hp[0];
        port = hp[1].parse_int().or(DEFAULT_PORT);
    }
    if (t.contains(HASH1)) {
        return Result<Url, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    if (target == "*") {
        if (method != "OPTIONS") {
            return Result<Url, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
        return url_of("http", name, port, t, what);
    }
    if (t.starts_with("http://".to_bytes()) || t.starts_with("https://".to_bytes())) {
        bytes rest = t.substr(7, t.size());
        if (t.starts_with("https://".to_bytes())) {
            rest = t.substr(8, t.size());
        }
        match (rest.index_of("/".to_bytes())) {
            case Some(int slash): {
                return url_of("http", name, port, rest.substr(slash, rest.size()), what);
            }
            case None: {
                return url_of("http", name, port, "/".to_bytes(), what);
            }
        }
    }
    if (t[0] != '/') {
        return Result<Url, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    return url_of("http", name, port, t, what);
}

// --- writing a request -----------------------------------------------------
/// Write `req` to `s`: the request line, its fields and its body, in **one**
/// `write`.
///
/// The framing is this function's and not the caller's: a chunked request
/// gets `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`, one with a body gets a
/// `Content-Length`, and one with an empty body and no chunking gets
/// **neither** -- a `Content-Length: 0` on a `GET` is noise, and a server
/// reads the absence as "no body", which is what it is.
///
/// `Host` is filled in from the URL if the caller has not set one, and
/// `User-Agent` likewise. `Connection: close` is added unless the caller has
/// said otherwise, because one call is one connection here; a caller driving
/// a kept-alive connection through this seam sets `connection` itself.
///
/// The target is written byte for byte from the URL. This function never
/// re-encodes what it was handed.
pub Result<void, Error> write_request(io.Stream s, Request req) {
    Headers h = req.head;
    if (!h.has("host")) {
        h.set("host", req.loc.host_header());
    }
    if (!h.has("user-agent")) {
        h.set("user-agent", USER_AGENT);
    }
    if (!h.has("connection")) {
        h.set("connection", "close");
    }
    if (req.framed) {
        h.set("transfer-encoding", "chunked");
        h.remove("content-length");
    } else if (req.payload.size() > 0) {
        h.set("content-length", req.payload.size().to_str());
        h.remove("transfer-encoding");
    } else {
        h.remove("content-length");
        h.remove("transfer-encoding");
    }
    bytes out = (req.verb + " " + req.loc.target() + " HTTP/" + req.ver).to_bytes();
    out.extend(CRLF);
    h.render(out, "request")?;
    out.extend(CRLF);
    if (req.framed) {
        chunk_into(out, req.payload);
    } else {
        out.extend(req.payload);
    }
    return write_all(s, out);
}

// --- reading a response ----------------------------------------------------
/// The response on `s` to a request that used `method`.
///
/// **`method` is not decoration.** Nothing in a response marks that it has no
/// body when it answers a `HEAD`; only the caller knows, and a client that
/// forgets hangs waiting for a body that is not coming.
///
/// Interim (1xx) responses are stepped over -- `103 Early Hints` arrives
/// unasked from real servers -- up to eight of them, after which the peer is
/// looping.
///
/// The framing rules are **not** the request's, and the difference is the
/// dangerous one: a request with neither framing field has no body, and a
/// response with neither has a body that ends when the connection closes.
/// Reusing one rule for both would give an empty body for every
/// HTTP/1.0-style response, or read the next request as a body on the server
/// side.
pub Result<Response, Error> read_response(io.Stream s, str method) {
    int interim = 0;
    while (true) {
        Response resp = read_one_response(s, method)?;
        if (resp.code >= 200 || resp.code == 101) {
            return Result<Response, Error>.Ok(resp);
        }
        interim = interim + 1;
        if (interim > MAX_INTERIM) {
            return Result<Response, Error>.Err(Error.TooLarge);
        }
    }
}

Result<Response, Error> read_one_response(io.Stream s, str method) {
    bytes raw = read_head(s)?;
    if (raw.size() == 0) {
        return Result<Response, Error>.Err(Error.Closed);
    }
    List<bytes> lines = head_lines(raw)?;
    List<str> start = status_line(lines[0])?;
    Headers h = parse_headers(lines, 1)?;
    Response resp = Response(start[1].parse_int().or(0), start[2], start[0], h);
    if (method == "HEAD" || bodiless(resp.code)) {
        return Result<Response, Error>.Ok(resp);
    }
    if (method == "CONNECT" && resp.code >= 200 && resp.code < 300) {
        return Result<Response, Error>.Err(Error.Framing);
    }
    int len = framing(h)?;
    if (len == -2) {
        resp.payload = read_chunked(s, MAX_BODY)?;
    } else if (len >= 0) {
        resp.payload = read_exact(s, len, "the response body")?;
    } else {
        resp.payload = read_to_end(s, MAX_BODY, "the response body")?;
    }
    return Result<Response, Error>.Ok(resp);
}

// The version, the status code and the reason phrase, as three strings. The
// phrase may hold spaces, so the line is cut at its first two spaces rather
// than split on every one; RFC 9112 allows the phrase to be empty, and a
// line with no phrase and no space after the code is accepted too, because
// some servers send one and there is nothing ambiguous about it.
Result<List<str>, Error> status_line(bytes line) {
    int first = -1;
    match (line.index_of(SP1)) {
        case Some(int i): {
            first = i;
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
    }
    str version = version_of(line.substr(0, first), "status line")?;
    bytes rest = line.substr(first + 1, line.size());
    bytes digits = rest;
    bytes phrase = [];
    match (rest.index_of(SP1)) {
        case Some(int i): {
            digits = rest.substr(0, i);
            phrase = rest.substr(i + 1, rest.size());
        }
        case None: {
        }
    }
    if (digits.size() != 3) {
        return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    for (int c in digits) {
        if (c < '0' || c > '9') {
            return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
    }
    int bad = bad_value_at(phrase);
    if (bad >= 0) {
        return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    str said = "";
    match (phrase.utf8()) {
        case Some(str t): {
            said = t;
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<List<str>, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
    }
    List<str> out = [version, ascii(digits), said];
    return Result<List<str>, Error>.Ok(out);
}

// --- writing a response ----------------------------------------------------
/// Write `resp` to `s` as the answer to `req`, in **one** `write`.
///
/// The framing fields are chosen here and written into `resp`, so a response
/// that has been sent carries the headers that went out:
///
///   - a bodiless status (1xx, 204, 304) gets neither framing field and no
///     body, whatever the handler set;
///   - a `HEAD` gets the fields the `GET` would have got, and no body;
///   - `set_chunked(true)` gets `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`, unless the
///     peer is 1.0, which has no chunked coding;
///   - everything else gets a `Content-Length`.
///
/// `keep_alive` is what `should_keep_alive` decided: false writes
/// `Connection: close`, and true tells a 1.0 client `Connection: keep-alive`,
/// which it needs, because 1.0 closes by default and the reuse it asked for
/// would otherwise silently not happen.
///
/// The status line always says `HTTP/1.1`: a server answers in the protocol
/// it speaks, not in the one it was told about. The reason phrase is the
/// response's own, so a relayed response keeps the phrase it arrived with.
pub Result<void, Error> write_response(io.Stream s, Request req, Response resp, bool keep_alive) {
    Headers h = resp.head;
    bool suppress = req.verb == "HEAD" || bodiless(resp.code);
    // `bodiless` and not `suppress`: a HEAD answer carries the framing fields
    // its GET would have carried and simply omits the body, so the chunked
    // choice is made as if the body were going out.
    bool chunked = resp.framed && !bodiless(resp.code) && req.ver != "1.0";
    if (bodiless(resp.code)) {
        h.remove("content-length");
        h.remove("transfer-encoding");
    } else if (chunked) {
        h.set("transfer-encoding", "chunked");
        h.remove("content-length");
    } else {
        h.set("content-length", resp.payload.size().to_str());
        h.remove("transfer-encoding");
    }
    if (!keep_alive) {
        h.set("connection", "close");
    } else if (req.ver == "1.0") {
        h.set("connection", "keep-alive");
    }
    bytes out = ("HTTP/1.1 " + resp.code.to_str() + " " + resp.phrase).to_bytes();
    out.extend(CRLF);
    h.render(out, "response")?;
    out.extend(CRLF);
    if (!suppress) {
        if (chunked) {
            chunk_into(out, resp.payload);
        } else {
            out.extend(resp.payload);
        }
    }
    return write_all(s, out);
}

/// Whether this connection can carry another request after this exchange.
///
/// Either side may say `close`. A 1.0 peer closes by default and reuses only
/// when it asked to; a 1.1 peer reuses by default.
///
/// It is part of the seam rather than of the server, because it is a question
/// about two messages and not about a socket: a proxy deciding whether to
/// reuse an upstream connection asks it too, and a test asks it of two
/// transcripts.
pub bool should_keep_alive(Request req, Response resp) {
    if (has_token(req.head.get("connection"), "close")) {
        return false;
    }
    if (has_token(resp.head.get("connection"), "close")) {
        return false;
    }
    if (req.ver == "1.0") {
        return has_token(req.head.get("connection"), "keep-alive");
    }
    return true;
}

// `body` appended to `out` as chunks, ending with the zero chunk and an
// empty trailer. `CHUNK` bytes at a time, so a body larger than that crosses
// several chunks and a decoder that handles only one is caught.
void chunk_into(bytes out, bytes body) {
    int off = 0;
    while (off < body.size()) {
        int take = body.size() - off;
        if (take > CHUNK) {
            take = CHUNK;
        }
        out.extend(hex_of(take).to_bytes());
        out.extend(CRLF);
        out.extend(body.substr(off, off + take));
        out.extend(CRLF);
        off = off + take;
    }
    out.extend("0".to_bytes());
    out.extend(CRLF);
    out.extend(CRLF);
}

Result<void, Error> write_all(io.Stream s, bytes out) {
    match (s.write(out)) {
        case Ok: {
            return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
        }
        case Err(io.Error e): {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(transport(e));
        }
    }
}

// --- the server ------------------------------------------------------------
/// What a server does with a request.
///
/// **A one-method interface is this language's callback** (§3.4,
/// `docs/closures-decision.md`): a type with a `Response handle(Request)`
/// method satisfies it structurally, with nothing declared, and so does a
/// **plain function's name** --
///
///     Response hello(http.Request req) { return http.text_response("hi"); }
///     http.serve(ln, hello, connections: 1);
///
/// -- which is the shape most handlers want and costs no type at all. A
/// handler that needs state is a type with fields and the same method. A
/// lambda will satisfy it too, when there are lambdas; nothing here changes
/// when they arrive, which is the whole point of the interface being the
/// callback type rather than a function type.
///
/// A handler may not fail: it returns a `Response`, and a handler that meets
/// a problem answers `http.text_response("...", status: 500)`. An interface
/// method returning a `Result` would make every server write the same two
/// lines turning it into a 500, and would leave open what to do with an
/// error nobody can answer.
pub interface Handler {
    Response handle(Request req);
}

/// Serve requests on one connection until the peer stops, either side says
/// `close`, or `max_requests` have been served. The number served.
///
/// **A malformed request is not this server's failure.** It is answered --
/// with `e.status()` and the sentence `e.to_str()` -- the connection is
/// closed, and this returns `Ok` with the count of requests that completed.
/// `Err` is for a failure that could not be answered on: a socket that is
/// gone, a timeout on an idle connection, a response this module could not
/// write.
///
/// A timeout, if `set_read_timeout` was called on `c`, ends the connection
/// quietly: a kept-alive connection that goes idle is the ordinary way one
/// ends, and there is nobody to tell.
pub Result<int, Error> serve_conn(net.Conn c, Handler h, int max_requests = MAX_REQUESTS) {
    int served = 0;
    while (max_requests < 0 || served < max_requests) {
        match (read_request(c)) {
            case Ok(Option<Request> maybe): {
                match (maybe) {
                    case None: {
                        return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
                    }
                    case Some(Request req): {
                        Response resp = h.handle(req);
                        bool again = should_keep_alive(req, resp) && (max_requests < 0 || served + 1 < max_requests);
                        write_response(c, req, resp, again)?;
                        served = served + 1;
                        if (!again) {
                            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            case Err(Error e): {
                return refuse(c, e, served);
            }
        }
    }
    return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
}

// Answer a refusal and stop. A transport failure is not answered: there is
// nothing to answer it on.
Result<int, Error> refuse(net.Conn c, Error e, int served) {
    match (e) {
        case Refused: {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
        }
        case TimedOut: {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
        }
        case Unreachable: {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
        }
        case Reset: {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
        }
        case InUse: {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
        }
        case NameNotFound: {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
        }
        case Syntax: {
        }
        case Framing: {
        }
        case TooLarge: {
        }
        case BadUrl: {
        }
        case Closed: {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
        }
        case Other: {
        }
        case CertificateUntrusted: {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
        }
        case CertificateExpired: {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
        }
        case HostnameMismatch: {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
        }
        case TlsFailed: {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
        }
        case InsecureRedirect: {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
        }
    }
    // A refusal is answered on a connection whose framing this end no longer
    // trusts, so it is the last thing written and the connection closes after
    // it -- which `Connection: close` says and the `false` below arranges.
    Response resp = text_response(e.to_str() + "\n", status: e.status());
    Request bare = bare_request();
    match (write_response(c, bare, resp, false)) {
        case Ok: {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
        }
        case Err: {
            return Result<int, Error>.Ok(served);
        }
    }
}

// The request a refusal is answered to when there is no parsed request to
// answer: 1.1, no method, so nothing is suppressed and no keep-alive is
// offered.
Request bare_request() {
    return Request("GET", Url("http", "", DEFAULT_PORT, "/", "", "/"), "1.1", headers());
}

/// Accept connections on `ln` and serve each one with `h`. The number of
/// connections served.
///
/// `connections` is how many to accept before returning; **-1 serves
/// forever**, and a number is what a test uses so that the loop ends.
/// `timeout_ms` is set on each connection as both a read and a write timeout,
/// so a peer that opens a connection and says nothing cannot hold this server
/// -- which matters, because of the next paragraph. `max_requests` bounds one
/// connection's turn for the same reason.
///
/// ### One connection at a time, and why
///
/// This loop serves one connection to its end before accepting the next.
/// That is a real limit -- one slow client delays every other -- and it is
/// what the language can express today, not a preference:
///
/// `spawn f(..)` **moves** its arguments, and only a value the current block
/// owns may be moved (§8.3). The connection half works: a `case Ok(net.Conn
/// c)` binding of a temporary may be moved, and the reference says so in as
/// many words. The **handler** half does not. `h` is a parameter, which is
/// borrowed and may not be moved; `clone(h)` would hand each connection a
/// different handler object, which is not what one handler means, and is
/// refused outright for a handler that owns a resource. There is no way to
/// give the same handler to two threads, because non-atomic refcounts are
/// sound only while one thread can reach a value (docs/concurrency-decision.md),
/// and that is the point rather than a gap to work around.
///
/// What would change it is a handler that is a **module constant** -- those
/// are immortal and shared, and every thread may read one (§8.3) -- or
/// channels: an accept loop that `send`s each moved `Conn` to a fixed set of
/// worker threads, each holding its own handler. Both are programs a caller
/// can write today on top of `serve_conn`, which is why `serve_conn` is
/// public and takes one connection. This module does not pick one for them.
///
/// `timeout_ms` and `max_requests` are the mitigation that is available, and
/// they are the reason they are arguments rather than constants.
pub Result<int, Error> serve(net.Listener ln, Handler h, int connections = -1, int timeout_ms = TIMEOUT_MS, int max_requests = MAX_REQUESTS) {
    int done = 0;
    while (connections < 0 || done < connections) {
        match (ln.accept()) {
            case Ok(net.Conn c): {
                // One connection's failure is not the server's. A peer that
                // hangs up in the middle of a response, or goes quiet until
                // the read timeout, ends that connection and nothing else --
                // a server that stopped accepting because one client did
                // would be a server any client could stop. `serve_conn` is
                // public for a caller who wants to see these.
                one_connection(c, h, timeout_ms, max_requests);
            }
            case Err(net.Error e): {
                return Result<int, Error>.Err(from_net(e));
            }
        }
        done = done + 1;
    }
    return Result<int, Error>.Ok(done);
}

// One accepted connection, to its end, with whatever went wrong on it kept
// on it. The `Conn` closes itself when this returns (§7.1).
void one_connection(net.Conn c, Handler h, int timeout_ms, int max_requests) {
    match (deadline(c, timeout_ms)) {
        case Ok: {
        }
        case Err: {
            return;
        }
    }
    match (serve_conn(c, h, max_requests: max_requests)) {
        case Ok: {
        }
        case Err: {
        }
    }
}

// Both timeouts, or the failure that stopped them being set. A connection
// with no deadline is a connection an idle peer can hold forever, and this
// server has one at a time.
Result<void, Error> deadline(net.Conn c, int timeout_ms) {
    match (c.set_read_timeout(timeout_ms)) {
        case Ok: {
        }
        case Err(net.Error e): {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(from_net(e));
        }
    }
    match (c.set_write_timeout(timeout_ms)) {
        case Ok: {
        }
        case Err(net.Error e): {
            return Result<void, Error>.Err(from_net(e));
        }
    }
    return Result<void, Error>.Ok;
}

// --- the client ------------------------------------------------------------
/// `GET url`, with the body read and the connection closed.
///
///     match (http.get("http://127.0.0.1:8080/health")) { ... }
///
/// There is no `post`, `put` or `delete` beside it: each would be `fetch`
/// with a string spelled in, one more name frozen, and a set of verbs that
/// is wrong the moment somebody needs `PROPFIND`. `get` earns its place
/// because a GET of a URL with no body and no headers is most of what a
/// client does; anything else is `http.request(method, url)` and `fetch`.
pub Result<Response, Error> get(str url) {
    return fetch(request("GET", url)?);
}

/// Make `req`, follow up to `redirects` redirects, and answer with the
/// response, its body already read and its connection already closed.
///
/// This is the `send(request)` an HTTP library usually calls `send`. It
/// cannot be called that: `send` is the channel builtin (§6.6) and the
/// language refuses a module function that shadows one.
///
/// **One call is one connection.** It sends `Connection: close`, reads the
/// answer and hangs up. There is no pool, so there is no idle connection to
/// go stale, no half-closed socket to hand to the next caller, and no
/// lifetime for a caller to get wrong. That costs a handshake per call and it
/// is the honest price.
///
/// ### Redirects: five hops, and never off the origin
///
/// `trust` is whom an `https` server must prove it is: the operating
/// system's roots by default, a CA file, or a pinned key (`lib/tls.m31`).
/// It has no value that skips the check, and it is ignored by `http` URLs.
///
/// `redirects` is how many `Location` hops to follow; **0 hands back the 3xx
/// itself**, which is what a caller who wants to decide for themselves
/// passes. The default is `MAX_REDIRECTS`.
///
/// **A redirect to another host or port is refused**, as is one from `https`
/// to `http` (`InsecureRedirect`); the single change followed is the same
/// host from `http` to `https`.
///
/// **The target is named in the error.** Following one would forward whatever the
/// caller set -- an `Authorization` field above all -- to a host they never
/// wrote down, and this client has no credential model, no cookie jar and no
/// header-stripping policy with which to make that safe. A caller who means
/// to cross hosts reads `Location` off the 3xx and calls `fetch` again,
/// which is the same number of lines and says what it is doing. Oro's client
/// does not follow redirects at all and gives this reason for it; five
/// same-origin hops is the smallest useful thing above that.
///
/// A `Location` that is an absolute path (`/other`) is resolved against the
/// current URL. A relative reference (`other`, `../other`) is refused:
/// resolving one is RFC 3986 §5's algorithm, with its dot-segment removal,
/// and a client that gets that subtly wrong sends a request somewhere
/// nobody asked for.
///
/// **301, 302 and 303 turn a non-GET into a GET with no body**, which is
/// what every browser and every other client does and what 303 means
/// outright. **307 and 308 keep the method and the body**, which is what
/// they were added to the standard to promise.
pub Result<Response, Error> fetch(Request req, int redirects = MAX_REDIRECTS, int timeout_ms = TIMEOUT_MS, tls.Trust trust = tls.Trust.SystemRoots) {
    Request cur = req;
    int hops = 0;
    while (true) {
        Response resp = exchange(cur, timeout_ms, trust)?;
        if (hops >= redirects || !is_redirect(resp.code)) {
            return Result<Response, Error>.Ok(resp);
        }
        cur = redirected(cur, resp)?;
        hops = hops + 1;
    }
}

bool is_redirect(int code) {
    return code == 301 || code == 302 || code == 303 || code == 307 || code == 308;
}

// One connection, one request, one response. An `https` URL puts a verified
// TLS connection over the socket first: the certificate has been proven
// before the first byte of the request is written.
Result<Response, Error> exchange(Request req, int timeout_ms, tls.Trust trust) {
    match (net.connect(req.loc.host(), req.loc.port())) {
        case Ok(net.Conn c): {
            deadline(c, timeout_ms)?;
            // A request is a message that waits for an answer, so there is
            // nothing for the kernel to coalesce it with.
            match (c.set_nodelay(true)) {
                case Ok: {
                }
                case Err(net.Error e): {
                    return Result<Response, Error>.Err(from_net(e));
                }
            }
            if (req.loc.scheme() == "https") {
                match (tls.connect_over(c, req.loc.host(), trust)) {
                    case Ok(tls.Conn secure): {
                        write_request(secure, req)?;
                        return read_response(secure, req.verb);
                    }
                    case Err(tls.Error te): {
                        return Result<Response, Error>.Err(from_tls(te));
                    }
                }
            }
            write_request(c, req)?;
            return read_response(c, req.verb);
        }
        case Err(net.Error e): {
            return Result<Response, Error>.Err(from_net(e));
        }
    }
}

// The request the next hop makes, or why this redirect is not followed.
Result<Request, Error> redirected(Request req, Response resp) {
    str where = "";
    match (resp.head.get("location")) {
        case Some(str v): {
            where = v.trim();
        }
        case None: {
            return Result<Request, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
        }
    }
    if (where == "") {
        return Result<Request, Error>.Err(Error.Syntax);
    }
    Url next = resolve(req.loc, where)?;
    if (req.loc.scheme() == "https" && next.scheme() != "https") {
        return Result<Request, Error>.Err(Error.InsecureRedirect);
    }
    // The one change of origin that is followed: the same host, `http` to
    // `https`, which is the upgrade nearly every site answers a plain request
    // with. Whatever the caller set went in the clear to that host already,
    // and now goes encrypted to it. Anything else -- another host, another
    // port on the same scheme -- is refused, for the reason `fetch` gives.
    bool upgrade = req.loc.scheme() == "http" && next.scheme() == "https" && next.host() == req.loc.host();
    if (!upgrade && (next.scheme() != req.loc.scheme() || next.host() != req.loc.host() || next.port() != req.loc.port())) {
        return Result<Request, Error>.Err(Error.BadUrl);
    }
    str method = req.verb;
    bytes body = req.payload;
    if (resp.code == 301 || resp.code == 302 || resp.code == 303) {
        if (method != "GET" && method != "HEAD") {
            method = "GET";
            bytes none = [];
            body = none;
        }
    }
    Headers h = req.head.copy();
    h.remove("host");
    if (body.size() == 0) {
        h.remove("content-length");
        h.remove("content-type");
        h.remove("transfer-encoding");
    }
    Request out = Request(method, next, req.ver, h);
    out.payload = body;
    out.framed = req.framed && body.size() > 0;
    return Result<Request, Error>.Ok(out);
}

// A `Location` against the URL it arrived at: an absolute `http://` URL, or
// an absolute path. Anything else is refused -- see `fetch`.
Result<Url, Error> resolve(Url base, str where) {
    bytes w = where.to_bytes();
    if (w.contains("://".to_bytes())) {
        return parse_url(where);
    }
    if (w.size() > 0 && w[0] == '/') {
        if (bad_target_at(w) >= 0) {
            return Result<Url, Error>.Err(Error.BadUrl);
        }
        return url_of(base.scheme(), base.host(), base.port(), w, "the Location " + quoted(w));
    }
    return Result<Url, Error>.Err(Error.BadUrl);
}
